<h2 id="10h-qigong-in-ytt">Qigong, Energy Cultivation and Relevant Aspects of TCM</h2>
<p style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.1em;color:#3D7A8A;">A 10-hour module within the UNI YOGA 500-Hour Yoga Teacher Training Course</p>

<div class="shared-story">
<p>This focused 10-hour module introduces trainee yoga teachers to the principles of Qigong and the foundations of Traditional Chinese Medicine that directly relate to energy cultivation. It is offered as part of the UNI YOGA 500-Hour Yoga Teacher Training Course and bridges two ancient Asian traditions that share a common concern: the cultivation, circulation and preservation of vital energy.</p>
</div>

<h3>Module Content</h3>
<ul class="features">
<li>Introduction to Qigong as a branch of Traditional Chinese Medicine</li>
<li>The concept of Qi and how it relates to prana in the yogic tradition</li>
<li>The meridian system, acupuncture points and the Five Elements</li>
<li>Foundational Qigong forms suitable for integration into yoga teaching</li>
<li>Internal work - using the Yi (mind) to guide the energy</li>
<li>Practical applications for yoga teachers working with energy and subtle body awareness</li>
<li>How TCM principles can inform and deepen a yoga practice</li>
</ul>

<h3>Who Is This For</h3>
<ul class="features">
<li>Trainee teachers enrolled in the UNI YOGA 500-Hour Yoga Teacher Training Course</li>
<li>Yoga practitioners who want to understand the energetic dimensions of their practice more deeply</li>
<li>Teachers looking to bridge yogic and Chinese traditions in their work</li>
</ul>

<p>This module is delivered as part of the UNI YOGA 500-Hour Yoga Teacher Training Course. For enrolment in the full training, please <a href="/contact/">get in touch</a>.</p>

<p style="margin-top:1em;"><a href="#register">Register interest in this programme</a></p>

